damaged_code:
```rust
struct Student {
    id: u64,
    name: String,
    // Admissions Context fields
    high_school_gpa: f64,
    sat_score: u32,
    // Alumni Affairs Context fields
    donation_history: Vec<Donation>,
    membership_level: String,
}
```

counterexample:
A deliberately shared identity mapping or narrow stable key contract (e.g. standard key structures) is legitimate. Explicitly separate context models joined through boundary mappings already preserve the distinction. Shared basic authentication identity belongs only where it is an intentional common contract, not a reason to share all business data.
```rust
struct BookingContext { cargo: Cargo }
struct BillingContext { payment: Payment }
```

fix:
Create context-owned representations with names, fields, and invariants appropriate to each vocabulary. Correlate them using the agreed identity and translate explicitly at integration boundaries; do not recreate a universal entity in the mapping layer. Preserve each context's required data and behavior during the split.
```rust
// Admissions Bounded Context
struct Prospect {
    id: u64,
    name: String,
    high_school_gpa: f64,
}

// Alumni Bounded Context
struct Alumnus {
    id: u64,
    donation_history: Vec<Donation>,
}
```
